hey guys hey going as you might be able  to tell I'm not currently in my  classroom I'm not actually even in  Australia right now I'm currently in  Uganda in a set of schools run by  something called the School for Life  Foundation about an hour's drive outside  of Kampala which is the capital city of  Uganda I'm here in the school holidays  because this is a school which has just  been kind of started up by this  Australian NGO it's actually set of  three schools two primary schools and a  secondary because you know in Australia  we kind of take for granted the access  we have to education and that we we kind  of treat it as a bit of a you know it's  a universal human right that in fact we  say it's illegal to not send your  children to school but that's not what  it's like in Africa and certainly in a  place like Uganda right now the region  that I'm in is quite rural so if you  have a look around you or around me  rather you can see I'm in this beautiful  lush land and it's actually you know one  of the things that surprised me about  coming to Uganda is just how incredibly  green it is and how everything just it  just grows and in fact agriculture is  the main industry of Uganda but I'm here  for a week really five days after you  take out travel because it takes about  22 hours to get here in 22 hours to fly  back I'm here to work with these  students and teachers and help them you  know become better at learning and  teaching mathematics which is kind of  the thing that I do everywhere that I go  and it's really been an eye-opening  experience to see the challenges that  children deal with here and it's quite I  mean it despite the fact that it's so  difficult to get to you know many of the  children here they walk to this school  from maybe three or three to five  kilometres away that's kind of a radius  that the school assets so they're waking  up really early in the morning to to  pump water and get get food into their  homes and then to come to school they  start around 7:30 here because it gets  pretty warm in the afternoon  and just the the desire that people have  for education because they see it as the  way outside of out of poverty it's  something that's deeply valued here  which I think this there's not as much  appreciation for when we have such ready  access to schools so it's been a real  delight I am I'm pretty jet-lagged  because there's a 7 hour difference here  but it's been fantastic working with the  teachers and the students and I look  forward to sharing more stuff so you can  learn about the great work that school  fly foundation is doing here  catch you later guys
 

